Title:
mhydeath

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This repository provides a tool/PoC for abusing the **mhyprotect** kernel driver (not mhyprot2) to terminate or disable security products, including AV/EDR/XDR and Windows Protected Processes.  
- It targets a common problem for attackers and researchers: security agents that resist termination via user-mode controls due to self-protection and PPL (Protected Process Light).  
- The core idea is leveraging a vulnerable/abusable third-party kernel driver to perform privileged operations (e.g., killing protected processes) from kernel context.  
- The README references related projects (e.g., Terminator, evil-mhyprot-cli) and includes a demo showing impact against CrowdStrike Falcon and Microsoft Defender.  
- Useful for red teams and offensive security researchers validating defensive hardening against BYOVD-style attacks; also relevant to blue teams for detection engineering and driver-blocking strategy.

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD (Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver) / driver abuse
- Windows kernel driver interaction (IOCTL-based control)
- Terminating AV/EDR/XDR agents and Protected Processes (PPL)
- Security product self-protection bypass via kernel primitives
- Operationalization/demo against real-world EDR (CrowdStrike) and Defender

Use Cases:
- Validating whether an environment is resilient to driver-based EDR tampering
- Testing EDR self-protection and PPL enforcement under kernel-level attack
- Building detections around suspicious driver loading and IOCTL patterns
- Assessing effectiveness of Microsoft driver block rules / WDAC policies

Keywords:
mhyprotect, BYOVD, vulnerable driver, Windows kernel, IOCTL, PPL, Protected Process Light, EDR bypass, AV tampering, XDR, CrowdStrike Falcon, Microsoft Defender, process termination, driver loading, kernel-mode, self-protection bypass, WDAC, driver blocklist, Terminator, evil-mhyprot-cli